# Money: From Barter to Modern Currency

> Money is whatever a society accepts as the common medium of
> exchange; its functions and its measure define modern economies.

[definition]
**Money** is anything that is generally accepted as a medium of
exchange and that simultaneously serves as a unit of value and a
store of value. Acceptance, not material, makes money.
[/definition]

## 1. The Barter Problem

Barter — goods for goods — requires a **double coincidence of
wants**: a weaver wanting wheat must find a farmer wanting cloth, at
the same time and place. Barter also lacks a common measure of value
(n goods create n(n−1)/2 exchange rates) and a store of divisible
value. Money dissolves all three defects.

## 2. Evolution of Money

| Stage | Example | Weakness solved |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Commodity money | Cattle, salt, shells, grain | None — lacked uniformity |
| Metallic money | Gold, silver coins | Gave durability, divisibility |
| Paper money | Currency notes, initially backed by gold | Convenience of carrying value |
| Credit money | Cheques, bills, bank deposits | Transfer without cash |
| Electronic money | UPI, cards, digital wallets | Instant, contactless transfer |

@diagram tree id="money-evolution" layout="tree" aria="Evolution of money through five stages"
  Money -> Commodity
  Money -> Metallic
  Money -> Paper
  Money -> Credit
  Money -> Electronic
  "Commodity" -> "Cattle, salt, shells"
  "Electronic" -> "UPI, cards, wallets"
@/diagram

## 3. Functions of Money

**Primary functions:**

1. **Medium of exchange** — splits barter's sale and purchase apart;
   the double coincidence problem disappears.
2. **Unit of value (measure)** — one common denominator prices
   everything, making accounts and contracts possible.

**Secondary functions:**

3. **Standard of deferred payments** — debts, salaries, and loans
   are set in money terms, not in goats.
4. **Store of value** — purchasing power carried across time.
5. **Transfer of value** — money moves value across persons and
   places instantly.

[tip]
Memory hook: "MUM-ST" — Medium, Unit, Standard of deferred payment,
Transfer — all resting on the Store of value function.
[/tip]

## 4. Types of Money

- **Commodity money** — value from the material itself (gold coin).
- **Fiat money** — value by government order (legal tender); modern
  currency notes, worthless as paper, priceless by decree.
- **Fiduciary money** — accepted on trust in the issuer (cheques,
  bank deposits), not legal tender but convertible.

**Characteristics of good money**: general acceptability,
durability, portability, divisibility, uniformity, stability of
value, scarcity (difficulty of counterfeit).

## 5. Money Supply Measures (India)

| Measure | Composition | Liquidity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| M1 | Currency with public + demand deposits with banks + other deposits with RBI | Highest |
| M2 | M1 + savings deposits with post office | |
| M3 | M1 + time deposits with banks — **broad money** | |
| M4 | M3 + total post office deposits | Lowest |

- **M1 is narrow money** — money ready to spend.
- **M3 is broad money** — includes less liquid term deposits; it is
  the headline measure of money supply in India.

[info title="High-powered money (monetary base)"]
**High-powered money (M0)** = currency held by the public + cash
reserves with banks + cash reserves with the RBI. The RBI controls
M0 directly; commercial banks multiply it into M1 and M3 through
credit creation.
[/info]

## 6. RBI and Currency Issue

The Reserve Bank of India is the sole issuer of currency notes in
India (one-rupee notes and coins are issued by the Government of
India, circulated through the RBI). Since **1956** India follows the
**minimum reserve system**: the RBI maintains a minimum reserve of
gold and foreign securities (₹200 crore, of which ₹115 crore in
gold) and can issue any quantity of currency against it — the system
that lets money supply grow with the economy.

[example]
A ₹500 note costs far less than ₹500 to print. It is fiat money: its
worth rests on RBI backing, government legal-tender status, and your
confidence that the shopkeeper will accept it tomorrow.
[/example]

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | One-line memory |
| --- | --- |
| Double coincidence | Barter's fatal flaw |
| Primary functions | Medium of exchange, unit of value |
| Secondary functions | Deferred payment, transfer of value |
| Store of value | Bridges present and future |
| Fiat money | Value by government order |
| M1 | Currency + demand deposits (narrow) |
| M3 | M1 + time deposits (broad) |
| M0 / high-powered | Currency + bank reserves with RBI |
| Minimum reserve system | Since 1956, ₹200 crore backing |

[summary]
Money evolved from cattle to UPI to solve barter's frictions, and it
works through five functions built on general acceptability. India
measures it as M1 through M4 — narrow to broad — atop the RBI-
controlled monetary base, issued since 1956 under the minimum
reserve system.
[/summary]
